e the reporting backend lives; per `dashboard.md` the Dashboard owns no data plane and exposes Kubernetes API only (no private component APIs), so the finance-facing stable API cannot live in the Dashboard. Distributed Workloads owns the GPU workload/quota domain and the Kueue→Prometheus metrics surface the metering builds on. See Health Warnings.\n- `Documentation` (for the docs-authoring epic) is taken verbatim from the canonical list; it is not in the strategy's Affected Components and was introduced by the HLR-4 work-product split.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Analysis |\n|-----|----------|---------|----------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E002 (computation), E003 (administrator viewing) | HLR-1 spans the report backend and its administrator-facing view; split along the component/team boundary (Distributed Workloads backend, Dashboard UI), not by priority. Both epics are P0. |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | E002 | `bundled` — CSV export and the stable API are presentation modes of the same report data and API surface that HLR-1's computation produces; the export is not a separately schedulable deliverable (splitting would build the same API/report model twice and add an artificial serialization edge). The P1 label reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E003 | `bundled` — the current-month-vs-Kueue-quota comparison renders in the same Dashboard admin view surface as HLR-1's report viewing; splitting would build the same view twice. The P1 label reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | Documentation work-product split (substantive content authoring: report semantics and export integration topics). Epic priority is P1 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter priority Major → P1), not P2; docs edges do not push priority upstream. |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections** (proceeded with available information per the parse step): no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table (the RHAISTRAT-9990 dependency appears only as a frontmatter link), no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Affected Components is a bare list without per-component change/owner detail; teams were inferred from architecture-context owning teams (`Distributed Workloads`, `RHOAI Dashboard`), and the documentation team was inferred as `RHOAI Documentation` (no architecture-context file exists for Documentation).\n- **Report-backend ownership ambiguity**: the strategy does not state which component hosts report computation and the stable API. Assigned to Distributed Workloads (rationale in Component mapping notes). If the metering strategy ships its own query API, E002's scope shifts toward consumption of that API — revisit when the investigation completes.\n- **Architecture tension on HLR-2**: a \"stable API for finance tooling\" is a new non-Kubernetes API surface not present in either component's architecture context. The exposure model must be confirmed during the investigation and implementation; flagged so reviewers do not read it as grounded.\n- **Docs priority vs HLR label**: HLR-4 is P2 but the docs epic is P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (Major → P1). Recorded for reviewers; not an inversion.\n- **Re-split trigger (E002)**: report computation + export API is one logical change over one surface; if it exceeds roughly one sprint of effort, re-split by extracting the stable API/CSV exposure as a follow-on epic once the internal report model is frozen — not before, since splitting earlier would build the same report model twice.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nExternal dependency with uncertain timing: the metering data schema owned by the in-flight RHAISTRAT-9990 strategy.\n\n1. Can the strategy deliver partial value without it? **Yes — risk-reduction validation.**\n2. Split:\n   - **Tier 1**: E001 (Investigation) — pins the consumption contract and decides whether reporting can build against the current schema draft. Independent of the metering strategy's landing date.\n   - **Tier 2**: E002, E003, E004 — gated on E001's outcome. If the draft schema is judged stable, E002 proceeds against it behind the consumption contract with final-schema conformance as an acceptance criterion (the tiered mitigation) rather than waiting for RHAISTRAT-9990 to land; if not, the gate's recorded fallback (versioned adapter, or deferral until finalization) applies.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":35483,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b40d7958395545f2","agent_id":"abfce0a9feb4636dc","session_id":"9b38e4b5-1cb5-4536-862e-6126a53124be","uuid":"3ada41cd-b2d6-41d9-9696-1aebb89448c3","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:03:20.457Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9018"}
